{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787060652,"nonce":"Nfqz1HA0nOwcoiFJGHT9VXV/g1M=","title":"There is no way to ask what happened in a namespace over a window, because every notification is triggered by one object","message":"**What is wrong.** Every notification oto can send is triggered by a change to a single object — an alert\nfired, a case was acked, a group's membership moved. There is no way to say \"tell me what happened in the\nobservability namespace in the last ten minutes, and don't bother me unless it was more than a trickle\".\nThat question has no subject: it is not about an alert, a case or a group, but about a set selected by\nproperties over a window.\n\n**The demonstration.** All eighteen reasons in `internal/notification/domain/reason.go:18-66` name a change\nto one thing. `notification_policies` (`db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:102`) selects WHICH object's\nchanges reach a channel; it cannot express a schedule, a window or a floor. `ReasonRepeat` is the closest\nthing and it still hangs off one group.\n\n**Why it matters.** The unit an operator wants for a busy namespace is a period, not an event — and today\nthe only way to approximate it is to receive every event and read them as a batch by eye. Evaluating this\nis cheap if it is done on a TICK: at the top of the window, count what matches with one query over rows\nalready stored. It is expensive only if evaluated event-driven, which needs durable per-window state and\ntimers. Tick evaluation also gives the floor for free, because the count is already in hand.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:102-149` (policies) · `internal/notification/domain/reason.go`\n· `internal/notification/service` (the evaluator) · `channel_threads` (`00011:150`) · the subject-kind ticket,\nwhich this depends on.\n\n**Scope.** large\n\n**Done when.**\n1. A policy may carry a schedule (a window length) and an optional floor, and both are absent by default —\n   a policy without them behaves exactly as today. A test asserts that.\n2. Evaluation is TICK-DRIVEN: at each window boundary, one query counts what matches the selector in that\n   window. No durable state beyond \"which window was last covered\". A test asserts a window is covered\n   exactly once even across a restart.\n3. Nothing is sent when the window is empty, or when the count is below the floor. A test asserts both.\n4. The subject is the pair `(policy_id, window_start)`, which is also the idempotency key — so a retry\n   cannot double-post. `subject_kind` gains a fourth value for it, named `digest`.\n5. A digest threads as ONE ongoing conversation per policy per channel, one reply per tick — not a new\n   thread per window. `UNIQUE (channel_id, subject_kind, subject_id)` needs no change to allow this.\n6. Alert-based and case-based policies gain NO schedule and NO floor. They fire immediately, every time.\n   Their noise is a signal to fix the Prometheus rule, and oto does not decide to be quiet about them.","files":null}]}